@namespace Croft.Web.Components
@using Croft.Web.Services
@* The rail-less "On this page" panel: at content widths where the rail is not shown the TOC and
   the tags become one panel under the hero with three states — the in-flow block (the TOC title,
   up to five entries, a "+N more…" button and the tags in a right-hand column), the pinned
   one-row bar (the scroll-spy's current section and a chevron) and the opened drawer (the full
   list scrolling inside at most 70vh, tags still on the right). wwwroot/js/post.js owns the
   states: pinning and shrinking, the row's title (read from the one scroll-spy's aria-current
   entry), the pull gesture on the handle and the closes. The list is the shared TableOfContents
   and the tags the shared TagPills — one source of truth for both. The states are a JavaScript
   enhancement: without them the block shows its whole list in place. Posts with fewer than two
   headings get no panel: their Tags panel
   stays after the text (the page's layout rules key on this component's presence).
   The root keeps the toc-inline hook because this block is what earlier layouts called the
   inline "On this page" — the pages' @container rules and the post tests locate it by that name. *@
@if (Headings is { Count: >= 2 })
{
    <section class="toc-inline">
        @* Named by its content, not an aria-label: "Contents" for assistive technology, then the
           current section post.js writes into the row, so the name always contains the visible
           text (axe label-content-name-mismatch, brief 15d item 1). *@
        <button type="button" class="toc-inline__row" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="toc-inline-body">
            <span class="visually-hidden">Contents</span>
            <span class="toc-inline__row-text"></span>
            <span class="toc-inline__handle" aria-hidden="true">
                <svg class="toc-inline__chevron" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20">
                    <path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" />
                </svg>
            </span>
        </button>
        <div class="toc-inline__body" id="toc-inline-body">
            <div class="toc-inline__list-column">
                <TableOfContents Headings="@Headings" Path="@Path" />
                @if (Headings is { Count: > 5 })
                {
                    <button type="button" class="toc-inline__more">+@(Headings.Count - 5) more…</button>
                }
            </div>
            @if (Tags is { Count: > 0 })
            {
                <div class="toc-inline__tags"><TagPills Tags="@Tags" /></div>
            }
        </div>
        @* The drawer's pull-up target (brief 07i item 2): a strip at the drawer's bottom edge
            with the grab bar in it. It exists only for the open state's gesture — the row's
            handle keeps its pull-down — and is decorative to assistive tech (the row button
            already closes), so it is aria-hidden and never focusable. *@
        <div class="toc-inline__strip" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="toc-inline__grab"></span>
        </div>
    </section>
    @* The scrim over the page below the opened drawer (brief 07i item 3): post.js sizes its top
        edge to the drawer's bottom; tapping it closes the drawer exactly as tapping outside
        always has. A sibling of the panel, below it in stacking order, so the drawer paints
        over it and its tap never counts as a tap inside the panel. It carries no colour: the
        alpha comes from the --scrim token on the theme (app.css), per the public pages' CSP
        (no style attributes; state sizes go through the CSSOM only). *@
    <div class="toc-inline__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The post's h2/h3 headings; the panel shows itself only for two or more — the
    /// same rule as the TOC it carries.</summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<Heading>? Headings { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The post's tags, in the panel's right-hand column.</summary>
    [Parameter] public IReadOnlyList<TagDto>? Tags { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The post page's own path, forwarded to TableOfContents so its links are path-qualified
    /// (a bare "#id" href would resolve against &lt;base href="/"&gt; and leave the page).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Path { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
